  "summary": "Dutch modernist architecture emphasizing functionalism, rationalism, and the integration of architecture with social reform.",
  "description": "It features simplified forms, open floor plans, and the use of modern materials like steel and concrete, developed as a response to traditional Dutch architecture. The movement emerged from the Amsterdam School and was influenced by international modernist movements while maintaining Dutch social housing principles.",
  "culturalContext": "Nieuwe Bouwen — Dutch functionalism (Nieuwe Bouwen = \"New Building\") — was the Netherlands' De Stijl-to-Dudok modernism: J.J.P. Oud's Rotterdam housing estates, Johannes Duiker's Zonnestraal sanatorium and Open Air School, Willem Marinus Dudok's Hilversum town hall, brise-soleil, roof terraces and social-housing idealism. Dutch rationalism (H.P. Berlage's brick groundwork) made its modernism humane and communal; the 2010s restoration of Zonnestraal canonized it as a UNESCO-tourism-grade monument of socially ambitious light.",
